R"VG(
# sunlost4.vg (D-05): the observatory's table of the sun's disc, a drifter's people's while it dimmed and went (`dark` 1-2): a heading, a
# note, the table (`@suntable`: a row a year, `sunsize` of `sunfrom` in the people's own parts) and a last line, in each age's way. A people
# keeps its own row (`ms.fmt`), the rows short (a table, not a sentence said six times), and its own share of the notes.
@apart
@root ms.row

genre.sunmeasure (words=26..200)
  (3 has:writer) {@cast writer}{ms.fmt}{ms.head.me}\n{ms.note} {ms.note2}\n{@suntable 6 ms.row}\n{ms.close}
  (2 has:writer) {@cast writer}{ms.fmt}{ms.head.me}\n{@suntable 7 ms.row}\n{ms.note} {ms.close}
  (2) {ms.fmt}{ms.head}\n{ms.note}\n{@suntable 6 ms.row}\n{ms.note2} {ms.close}
  (2) {ms.fmt}{ms.head}\n{@suntable 5 ms.row}\n{ms.note} {ms.note2}
  (1 has:writer) {@cast writer}{ms.fmt}{ms.head}\n{@suntable 8 ms.row}\n{ms.close.me}

# ---- the table's way: a people's observatory keeps its own (an attribute the rows read)

ms.fmt (people=2)
  (age=medieval) {=.row:ord}
  (age=medieval) {=.row:of}
  (age=medieval) {=.row:parts}
  (age=industrial) {=.row:parts}
  (age=industrial) {=.row:of}
  (age=industrial) {=.row:year}
  (age>=modern) {=.row:units}
  (age>=modern) {=.row:year}
  (age>=futuristic) {=.row:of}

ms.row
  (.row=ord) The {#y:ord} year: {sunsize}.
  (.row=of) {#y:digits}, {sunsize} of {sunfrom}.
  (.row=parts) {#y:digits}: {sunsize} parts.
  (.row=year) Year {#y:digits}: {sunsize}.
  (.row=units) {#y:digits}: {sunsize} units.

# ---- the heading, unsigned: the object's or the observatory's

ms.head (people=2)
  (age=medieval medium=codex) The measure of the sun's face at [noon on the longest day|the noon of midsummer|the shortest noon shadow of the year], taken through the sighting-tube on [the roof|the high wall|the tower] of {holyplace}, in parts of the first measure.
  (age=medieval medium=codex) Here the watchers of {$home} wrote the width of the sun each year, as their teachers taught them, and did not leave off.
  (age=medieval medium=codex) A book of [vellum|bark leaves|thin boards], its first pages the stars of {festival}, its last pages the sun, in a [tired|careful|crabbed] hand.
  (age=medieval medium=tablet) Pressed into the clay in two rows, the year and the number, under a sun-sign drawn smaller at each line.
  (age=medieval medium=tablet) The count of the sun's face, made at {holyplace} at the noon of the longest day.
  (age=medieval medium=tablet) A tablet from the watchers' house, its corners [chipped|baked hard|worn round by hands]: the sun's width, year by year.
  (age=medieval medium=plate) On the back of the plate, below the rete, a table cut small: a year, and a number for each.
  (age=medieval medium=plate) Engraved round the rim of the plate, in the maker's smallest hand, the width of the sun by the years:
  (age=industrial medium=printed) Table of the solar diameter, as measured at the {city} Observatory at noon on the longest day, in parts of the first measure.
  (age=industrial medium=printed) The diameter of the sun: the Observatory's yearly figures, printed for the information of the public.
  (age=industrial medium=printed) A printed sheet, the Observatory's seal at the head, the figures in a column down the middle and a [black|plain|double] rule under them.
  (age=industrial medium=glass) A glass plate, the sun a pale circle in the middle of it, very small; on the sleeve, in ink, a table.
  (age=industrial medium=glass) The plate shows the sun's disc against a ruled grid; the observers wrote its widths along the edge of the sleeve.
  (age=industrial medium=glass) A plate in a [paper|card|cloth] sleeve; the sun on it no bigger than a seed, and the figures in pencil below.
  (age=industrial medium=codex) A notebook in a careful hand, its first pages the night sky and its last the sun:
  (age=industrial medium=codex) The observer's own book: the sun's width at noon, measured with the great glass, and nothing else in it at the end.
  (age=industrial medium=codex) A ledger meant for the Observatory's coal, turned over and used for the sun.
  (age=modern medium=printed) Solar angular diameter, annual mean, the {city} station: in units of the first year of the series.
  (age=modern medium=printed) The bulletin of the solar series, as issued to the press every year until the press stopped.
  (age=modern medium=glass) A plate from the station's solar camera, the disc small in a field of grey; the series typed on a card in its sleeve.
  (age=modern medium=drive) The station's solar series: the disc measured every noon, a mean for every year.
  (age=modern medium=drive) Series file, the sun's angular size from the {city} instruments, the first year's value taken as the unit.
  (age=modern medium=drive) A file named for the sun, [the first in the folder|the only one still opened|copied twice]: its yearly means.
  (age=futuristic medium=drive) Measurement series: the primary's angular diameter, from the year the drift began.
  (age=futuristic medium=drive) The array's yearly means of the sun's disc, as logged, with the first year's mean as the unit.
  (age=futuristic medium=crystal) The record of the sun's width, kept in the crystal so that it would outlast the instruments.
  (age=futuristic medium=crystal) The primary's disc, year on year, as the halls' instruments saw it.
  (age=futuristic) The solar series of {$home}, kept [to the end|without a gap|by the night shift] as it was kept from the first.
  (age=farfuture) The measure of the light that was ours, kept as the old ones kept it, in their parts.
  (age=farfuture) The width of the sun, each year of its going: a small thing to keep, and kept.

# ---- the heading, signed by the observer (`@cast writer`)

ms.head.me (people=2)
  (age=medieval medium=codex) I, {$me:name}, keeper of the shadow-staff of {holyplace}, write here the sun's width as the staff gives it, in parts of the first measure.
  (age=medieval medium=codex) The book of the sun's measure, kept at {$home} by {$me:name} and by those before.
  (age=medieval medium=codex) These are the measures of {$me:name}, who watched the sun from [the roof|the tower|the high wall] of {holyplace} through every noon of the longest day.
  (age=medieval medium=tablet) {$me:name} made this at {$home}, from the shadow of the staff at noon: the year, and the sun's width.
  (age=medieval medium=tablet) The count of the sun, pressed by {$me:name} at {holyplace}, for whoever keeps the count after.
  (age=medieval medium=tablet) {$me:name} of the watchers' house pressed this at noon, [with cold hands|before the light went|on the longest day].
  (age=medieval medium=plate) Cut on the back of the plate by {$me:name}, who made it: the widths of the sun, for whoever turns it over.
  (age=medieval medium=plate) On the plate's back {$me:name} cut a table, small, as if ashamed of it: the year and the width.
  (age=industrial medium=printed) The solar diameter, by {$me:name}, observer at {$home}: the yearly figures, in parts of the first measure.
  (age=industrial medium=printed) Report of {$me:name}, observer, on the continued diminution of the solar diameter, with the table.
  (age=industrial medium=glass) On the sleeve of the plate, in {$me:name}'s ink: the sun's widths, plate by plate.
  (age=industrial medium=glass) {$me:name} exposed this plate at noon and measured the disc on it, as on every plate before.
  (age=industrial medium=codex) {$me:name}'s observing book. The sun's width at noon, the longest day, every year since it began.
  (age=industrial medium=codex) The private book of {$me:name}, observer, in which the figures are written before they are printed.
  (age=modern medium=printed) Solar series, {$home}. Compiled by {$me:name}. Units: the first year of the series.
  (age=modern medium=drive) Solar series, kept by {$me:name} at {$home}. The disc at noon, as a yearly mean.
  (age=modern medium=drive) {$me:name}'s working copy of the solar series, with the comments left in.
  (age=modern medium=glass) The station's last solar plate, the series written on its card in {$me:name}'s hand.
  (age=futuristic) {$me:name}, keeper of the solar series at {$home}: the primary's disc, the yearly means.
  (age=futuristic medium=crystal) Set down by {$me:name} in the crystal, so that it is kept when the rest is not: the sun's width, year by year.
  (age=farfuture) {$me:name} kept the measure, as it was kept before, in the old parts.
  (age=farfuture) {$me:name}, who took the measure in its last years, left it here.

# ---- the note: the observer as the disc shrinks (dark 1 dimming, 2 going)

ms.note (share=0.5)
  (dark=1 age=medieval) Each year the staff's shadow falls softer, and the ring it draws on the floor is smaller.
  (dark=1 age=medieval) The elders say the sun is only tired. The staff does not say so.
  (dark=1 age<=industrial) I measured it [three|four|five] times this year, and [three times|every time] it came out less.
  (dark=1) The measure is the same measure; it is the sun that has [changed|grown less|gone from us a little].
  (dark=1 age=medieval) The priests asked me to measure again, as if the sun would grow back for a second asking.
  (dark=1 age=medieval) The ring of the noon shadow now falls inside the mark my teacher cut, by [a finger|two fingers|a thumb's width].
  (dark=1 age>=industrial) The figures are corrected for the air and the heat of the instrument; the fall remains.
  (dark=1 age>=industrial) Every observatory that has written to us gives the same figures, within [a part|half a part|the error of the instrument].
  (dark=1 age>=industrial) The [public asks|newspapers ask|council asks] whether the figures are a mistake. They are not a mistake.
  (dark=1 age>=modern) The decline is smooth, and it is [not slowing|speeding up|faster than the models].
  (dark=1) The {crop} in the fields below the {ms.place} came in [late|thin|half-green] again, and the numbers say [why|by how much|what the farmers feel].
  (dark=1 sunway=wanderer) {sunway.thing} stands nearer the sun at every measure, and brighter.
  (dark=1 sunway=wanderer) At noon {sunway.thing} can be seen beside the sun with the bare eye now; nobody living saw that before.
  (dark=1 sunway=intruder) {sunway.thing} rises earlier each year, and the sun's width falls as it grows.
  (dark=1 sunway=dark) The stars near the sun are not where the old tables put them; the sun's width falls all the same.
  (dark=1 sunway=dark) The old star tables are wrong near the sun, and wronger every year, as if the sky were bending.
  (dark=1 sunway=dance) {sunway.thing} is further off each year, and ours goes with it.
  (dark=1 sunway=dance) The two suns drift apart at every measure; ours is the one that shrinks.
  (dark=2 age=medieval) The sun is now no wider than a thumbnail held at arm's length, and the staff barely throws a shadow.
  (dark=2 desert=0) The sea is frozen from the shore, and still I go up at noon to measure what is left.
  (dark=2 desert=1) The wells are frozen in their stones, and still I go up at noon to measure what is left.
  (dark=2 age<=industrial) By the next measure there will be no shadow to read; I will measure it against [the stars|the fixed stars|the marks on the tube].
  (dark=2) It is hard to say where the disc ends now; I give the width where [the light stops|the glow fades|the dark begins].
  (dark=2 age>=industrial) The instruments were made for a sun this size only at their [finest setting|last notch|very edge].
  (dark=2 age>=modern) Below a part the series will be meaningless; [every year brings it nearer|it is coming|we will know when it gets there].
  (dark=2) Nobody comes up to see the figures any more. I write them down because [they are true|someone should|it is my work].
  (dark=2) The snow falls out of a clear sky now, [at noon|even at noon|at the hour of the measure], and the measure is taken through it.
  (dark=2) The air itself is falling as snow on the [roof|steps|instrument], and I sweep it off before every reading.
  (dark=2 age>=industrial) The dome is iced shut; we measure from the [door|roof|yard] with [the small glass|a hand instrument|the old tube] now.
  (dark=2) It is as dark at noon as it used to be at [dusk|the end of the day|lamp-lighting].
ms.note2 (share=0.6)
  The first measure was taken when the sun was whole, and it is the measure of all the rest.
  The parts are the parts of the first year: what the sun was, cut into {ms.parts}.
  (age=medieval) The staff stands where it always stood; I have checked it against the old marks.
  (age=medieval) My teacher took the first measure. I did not think I would take the last.
  (age=medieval) The figures are cut deep so that the frost does not take them.
  (age>=industrial) The method is unchanged since the first figure; [only the result changes|the sun is what has changed|nothing else has moved].
  (age>=industrial) The figures were checked by [a second observer|two observers|the night assistant] each year.
  (dark=2 age=medieval) The sighting-tube frosts over at every reading and is warmed in the hand between them.
  (dark=2 age>=industrial) The eyepiece frosts over at every reading and is cleaned with a warm cloth between them.
  (dark=1) In the fields they say the summer is short. Here we can say by how much.
  (dark=1 age>=modern) The models agreed with the first three years. They no longer have to.
  (dark=2) There is less to measure, and more care in [measuring it|every reading|writing it down].
  (dark=2) We take turns at the noon reading so that nobody has to stand in the cold too long.

# the parts' name, without its number (the first measure is the whole: its number is in the rows)
ms.parts
  parts
  equal parts
  the old parts

# where the observer works, as a place below the fields
ms.place
  (age=medieval) watchers' house
  (age>=industrial) observatory
  (age>=modern) station

# ---- the last line

ms.close (share=0.6)
  (dark=1) The measure will be taken again next year, and every year after.
  (dark=1 age=medieval) {god.call}.
  (dark=1) Whoever reads this: the figures are true. Do not say we were mistaken.
  (dark=1 age>=industrial) Published for the information of the public, without comment.
  (dark=1 age>=industrial) Observations continue.
  (dark=1) Next year's figure will go below this one, and we will write it.
  (dark=2) I do not think there will be [another row|a row after this|another year to write].
  (dark=2) If it goes below a part, I will write [nothing|a blank|a mark for none], and that will be the measure.
  (dark=2 age=medieval) {god.call}, and keep the staff standing.
  (dark=2 age>=industrial) The series closes when the disc cannot be told from a star.
  (dark=2 age>=modern) Series continues while there is a disc.
  (dark=2) We are going down to {hall.below} after this measure. The [staff|tube|great glass] stays where it is.
  (dark=2) The next measure will be taken from below, if it can be taken at all.
ms.close.me (people=3)
  (dark=1) {$me:name}, keeper of the measure.
  (dark=1) Measured and written by {$me:name}, who will measure again.
  (dark=1) {$me:name}, at the {ms.place}, [in the cold of the morning|before the noon reading|by the lamp].
  (dark=2) {$me:name}, who measured it last.
  (dark=2) The last of the measures, by {$me:name}, before the halls.
  (dark=2) {$me:name}, with the frost on the page.
)VG"
